#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231810} :activity/id #uuid "60204b9d-c0a9-5524-bd30-c8accd8e6729" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049479 :topic/id #uuid "f868b857-f4b2-5b40-8da2-046b9db36a57" :topic/math-academy-id 6721} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231810 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231984 :tutorial/content "We know how to find a Fourier-series particular solution $y_p$ for a periodically forced linear ODE. In this topic, the new step is how to use $y_p$ to satisfy an initial condition.\n\nRecall that once a particular solution $y_p$ is chosen, the full solution has the form $$y = y_c + y_p,$$ where $y_c$ solves the corresponding homogeneous equation.\n\nNow, suppose the IVP is given at $x=x_0{:}$ $$y(x_0)=y_0.$$ Then, $$y_0 = y_c(x_0)+y_p(x_0).$$\n\nA useful simplification is to choose the particular solution so that $$y_p(x_0)=0.$$ In that case, the initial condition becomes $$y_0 = y_c(x_0),$$ so we can solve for the constant(s) in $y_c$ immediately (without evaluating any Fourier sums at $x_0$).\n\nOn the next slide, we’ll apply this idea in an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "4a6bfce3-0ee4-5ea7-905f-461e3f15500e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14713 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1d2d573d-acda-5605-a740-470ab9c3110a" :step/math-academy-id 607581 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231811}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231811 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231993 :tutorial/content "For example, consider the initial value problem $$y'-2y = f(x), \\qquad y(0)=3,$$ where $f(x)$ is a $2$-periodic function. Suppose we choose the forcing (and hence the Fourier-series particular solution) so that $$y_p(0)=0.$$\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation: $$y' - 2y = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y_c = Ae^{2x}.$$\n\nA $2$-periodic forcing function can be written using harmonics of frequency $\\pi$, so we may write the particular solution in the form $$y_p(x)=\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\left(a_n\\cos(n\\pi x)+b_n\\sin(n\\pi x)\\right).$$ Thus the full solution is $$y(x)=Ae^{2x}+\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\left(a_n\\cos(n\\pi x)+b_n\\sin(n\\pi x)\\right).$$\n\nNow apply $y(0)=3$ and use $y_p(0)=0$: $$\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A{e}^{2(0)}+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 3 & =A(1)+0 \\\\ A & =3.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, $$y(x)= 3e^{2x}+\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\left(a_n\\cos(n\\pi x)+b_n\\sin(n\\pi x)\\right).$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ae6c43bc-e554-5975-9433-c7d67c2adbdd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14804 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "48c86314-12fc-57dd-bb24-3dab5839b9c1" :step/math-academy-id 607582 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231812}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231812 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231418 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231291 :question/id #uuid "b7a81b9e-d12c-55c7-b755-8f4e5b8115c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-21859"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "742531ae-be2d-5fc8-8ef8-4f745c93bacd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049288}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365945}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order IVPs Using Fourier Series"} :step/id #uuid "26e21ed5-3121-54a7-8a6b-1264c940acaa" :step/math-academy-id 607583 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231813}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231813 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231989 :tutorial/content "When we solve a second-order IVP using Fourier series, we still represent the solution in the form $$y = y_c + y_p,$$ but since the homogeneous solution has *two* constants, we use *two* conditions: $$y(0)=y_0 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad y'(0)=y_1$$\n\nBefore we start, we can use the forcing series properties to simplify our work. For a second-order equation of the form $$y'' + \\omega^2 y = f(x)$$ with no $y'$ (damping) term, the particular solution can be simplified. Here, we have two cases:\n\n- **Case 1:** $f(x)$ is odd.\n\nIf $f(x)$ is *odd* and the equation has the form $y''+\\omega^2 y=f(x)$, then the particular solution is also *odd*, so it is a sine series: $$y_p(x)=\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right)$$ This immediately gives $y_p(0)=0,$ so the condition $y(0)=y_0$ determines the cosine constant in $y_c$ without evaluating any infinite sum at $0$.\n- **Case 2:** $f(x)$ is even.\n\nIf $f(x)$ is *even* and the equation has the form $y''+\\omega^2 y=f(x)$, then the particular solution is also *even*, so it is a cosine series: $$y_p(x)=\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right)$$ Here, the derivative is a sine series: $$y_p'(x)=-\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{a_n n\\pi}{L} \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right)$$ This immediately gives $y_p'(0)=0,$ so the condition $y'(0)=y_1$ determines the sine constant in $y_c$ without evaluating any infinite sum at $0$.\n\nIn both cases, the key idea is that evaluating at $x=0$ (and differentiating once) lets the initial conditions pick off the constants in $y_c$ as cleanly as possible.\n\nLet's see how this works in practice on the next slide." :tutorial/id #uuid "c8c0ab68-168a-57f1-9ab6-13bd9d042d32" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14781 :tutorial/title "Second-Order IVPs"} :step/id #uuid "ffe83dea-ed44-5451-a483-e07ad7977f77" :step/math-academy-id 607586 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231814}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231814 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231990 :tutorial/content "Consider the initial value problem $$y''+ 7y = f(x), \\qquad y(0)=3,\\quad y'(0)=0$$ where $f(x)$ is an even, $2\\pi$-periodic function. Since the forcing is even and the differential operator contains no $y'$ term, we represent the particular solution as a cosine series: $$y_p(x)=\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n\\cos(nx)$$\n\nNext, we solve the homogeneous equation $y''+7y=0.$ The characteristic equation is $\\lambda^2+7=0,$ so $$y_c=A\\cos(\\sqrt{7}x)+B\\sin(\\sqrt{7}x).$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Differentiating the total solution gives $$y'(x)=-A\\sqrt{7}\\sin(\\sqrt{7}x)+B\\sqrt{7}\\cos(\\sqrt{7}x)-\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty n a_n\\sin(nx).$$\n\nUsing the derivative condition $y'(0)=0{:}$ $$\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}(0) & =-A\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}(0)+B\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}(0)-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}(0) \\\\ 0 & =0+B\\sqrt{7}-0 \\\\ B & =0\\end{aligned}$$ Note that because the total solution is an even function, its derivative is an odd function, which must vanish at $x=0.$\n\nNext, we apply $y(0)=3.$ With $B=0,$ the total solution is $$y(x)\\sim A\\cos(\\sqrt{7}x)+\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n\\cos(nx).$$\n\nApplying $y(0)=3$ and noting that $\\cos(0)=1,$ we have $$\\begin{aligned}3 & =A+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} \\\\ A & =3-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the solution can be written as $$y(x)\\sim \\cos(\\sqrt{7}x)\\left(3-\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n\\right) +\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n\\cos(nx).$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "edbe311b-6f4f-5c28-96fa-394b23f30390" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14782 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example of a Second-Order IVP"} :step/id #uuid "3b6f9bb1-ddc0-5924-bda7-a089e2b187ec" :step/math-academy-id 607587 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231815}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231815 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231419 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231293 :question/id #uuid "97290d74-457b-522d-991d-7891e5a01d28" :question/math-academy-id "e-21876"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81f5380b-7a7d-52c5-a530-3768e7cf3143" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365960}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs Using Fourier Series"} :step/id #uuid "3a843d13-ef46-5939-817d-692b8c15b4c0" :step/math-academy-id 607588}] :activity/title "Solving IVPs Using Fourier Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186231521}]}
